NNAAMMEE
     vvmmssttaatt — report statistics about kernel activities

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     vvmmssttaatt [--ffiimmssttvvzz]
     vvmmssttaatt [--cc _c_o_u_n_t] [--MM _c_o_r_e] [--NN _s_y_s_t_e_m] [--ww _w_a_i_t] [_d_i_s_k _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     vvmmssttaatt reports certain kernel statistics kept about process, virtual mem‐
     ory, disk, trap, and CPU activity.  The default behavior is to print a
     one-line summary of these statistics.  The --cc and --ww flags may be used to
     continually report summaries.

     The options are as follows:

     --cc _c_o_u_n_t
             Repeat the display _c_o_u_n_t times.  The first display is for the
             time since a reboot and each subsequent report is for the time
             period since the last display.  If no _w_a_i_t interval is specified,
             the default is 1 second.

     --ff      Report on the number of fork(2), __tfork(3), and vfork(2) system
             calls as well as kernel thread creations since system startup,
             and the number of pages of virtual memory involved in each.

     --ii      Report on the number of interrupts taken by each device since
             system startup.

     --MM _c_o_r_e
             Extract values associated with the name list from the specified
             core instead of the running kernel.

     --mm      Report on the usage of kernel dynamic memory listed first by size
             of allocation and then by type of usage.

     --NN _s_y_s_t_e_m
             Extract the name list from the specified system instead of the
             running kernel.

     --ss      Display the contents of the _u_v_m_e_x_p structure (see uvm_init(9)),
             giving the total number of several kinds of paging related events
             which have occurred since system startup.

     --tt      Report on the number of page in and page reclaims since system
             startup, and the amount of time required by each.

     --vv      Print more verbose information.

     --ww _w_a_i_t
             Pause _w_a_i_t seconds between each display.  If no repeat _c_o_u_n_t is
             specified, the default is infinity.

     --zz      When used with --ii, also list devices which have not yet generated
             an interrupt.

     By default, vvmmssttaatt displays the following information just once:

     procs   Information about the numbers of processes in various states.

             r     in run queue
             s     sleeping

     memory  Information about the usage of virtual and real memory.

             avm   active virtual pages
             fre   size of the free list

     page    Information about page faults and paging activity.  These are av‐
             eraged each five seconds, and given in units per second.

             flt   page faults
             re    page reclaims (simulating reference bits)
             pi    pages paged in
             po    pages paged out
             fr    pages freed
             sr    pages scanned by clock algorithm

     disks   Disk transfers per second.  Typically paging will be split across
             the available drives.  The header of the field is the first char‐
             acter of the disk name and the unit number.  If more than two
             disk drives are configured in the system, vvmmssttaatt displays only
             the first two drives.  To force vvmmssttaatt to display specific
             drives, their names may be supplied on the command line.

     traps   Trap/interrupt rate averages per second over last 5 seconds.

             int   device interrupts per interval (including clock interrupts)
             sys   system calls per interval
             cs    CPU context switch rate (switches/interval)

     cpu     Breakdown of percentage usage of CPU time.

             us    user time for normal and low priority processes
             sy    system time
             id    CPU idle

FFIILLEESS
     /bsd                              default kernel image
     /dev/kmem                         default memory file

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The command vvmmssttaatt --ww 55 will print what the system is doing every five
     seconds; this is a good choice of printing interval since this is how of‐
     ten some of the statistics are sampled in the system.  Others vary every
     second and running the output for a while will make it apparent which are
     recomputed every second.

